**user**:
> I'm trying to use PyCall in Julia, but pycall doesn't seem to have access to the same modules that my normal python does. How can I install a module (here, "pytz") so PyCall lines will see it?
**assistant**:
> When using PyCall in Julia, it doesn't automatically use your existing Python environment; instead, it can use its own Python installation. To ensure PyCall sees the same Python modules as your usual environment, you have a few options:
> 
> 1. **Use Your Existing Python Environment:**
>    You can configure PyCall to use the Python version where your desired modules are installed. Set the `ENV["PYTHON"]` variable to the path of your preferred Python executable before you add or build PyCall:
> 
>    ```julia
>    ENV["PYTHON"] = "/path/to/your/python"
>    using Pkg
>    Pkg.build("PyCall")
>    ```
> 
>    Replace `"/path/to/your/python"` with the path to your Python executable (you can find it with `which python` on Linux/Mac or `where python` on Windows, if it's on your PATH).
> 
> 2. **Install Packages in PyCall's Default Python:**
> 
>    If you want to use the default Python environment that PyCall sets up, you can add Python packages using PyCall's built-in package manager. Start Julia and run the following commands:
> 
>    ```julia
>    using Pkg
>    Pkg.add("PyCall")
>    using PyCall
>    pyimport("pytz")  # This will fail if pytz is not yet installed
> 
>    # To install a package
>    run(`$(PyCall.python) -m pip install pytz`)
>    ```
> 
> By following these steps, you should be able to access "pytz" and other modules through PyCall in Julia. Let me know if you run into any issues!
